To configure the BGP neighbor while hiding your true ASN (65535) and backbone AS details, use the following commands. Replace `<virtual-as>` with the desired AS number to present to the peer (e.g., 65001), `<group-name>` with your BGP group, and `<neighbor-ip>` with the peer's IP address:

```bash
configure router bgp
    group "<group-name>"
        neighbor <neighbor-ip>
            local-as <virtual-as> private no-prepend-global-as
```

### Explanation:
- **`local-as <virtual-as>`**: Advertises a virtual AS number (`<virtual-as>`) instead of your real ASN (65535).
- **`private`**: Hides the virtual AS from the AS_PATH in routes received from the peer.
- **`no-prepend-global-as`**: Prevents prepending your true ASN (65535) to the AS_PATH in routes advertised to the peer.

This ensures the peer only sees the virtual AS in the AS_PATH, masking your real ASN and backbone details.
