To automatically tag outgoing BGP routes with a metric of 15000 only when the original route lacks a metric, use a route policy to conditionally set the metric and apply it as an export policy to relevant BGP groups or neighbors:

```cli
/configure router policy-options
begin
policy-statement "SET_METRIC_15000"
    entry 10
        from
            metric not present
        action accept
            metric set 15000
    exit
    entry 20
        action accept
    exit
commit
exit

/configure router bgp
group "YOUR_GROUP"  # Replace with your BGP group name
    export "SET_METRIC_15000"
exit
```

**Explanation:**  
- The policy `SET_METRIC_15000` checks if a route has no existing metric (`metric not present`).  
- **Entry 10:** Sets the metric to `15000` for routes without a metric, then accepts them.  
- **Entry 20:** Accepts all other routes (with existing metrics) unchanged.  
- Apply this policy as an **export** policy to your BGP group/neighbor to influence outbound route advertisements.  

**Verify:**  
After applying, check routes advertised to neighbors:  
```cli
show router bgp neighbor <IP> advertised-routes
